From: Chokri Hammedi <52697989+blue0x1@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] loader: short PS4 CPU sites jump through a relay

An AVX2 instruction of four bytes that was not rewritten when its module
loaded (its function has an indirect branch, so its neighbors cannot move)
had no room for a five-byte jump and faulted every time it ran. In Hollow
Knight: Silksong the FMOD mixer runs such instructions in its loops: about
200000 faults a second, a whole core in the kernel, crackling sound and 30
to 50 FPS.

Such a site now gets the first four bytes of a jump. The fifth byte is the
first byte of the next instruction, which stays as it is, since something
may jump to it. That byte is the top of the displacement, so the jump lands
in a 16 MB window the byte selects; a relay allocated in that window jumps
on to the replacement. When the next instruction is a missing one too, it
gets its jump first, so the byte no longer changes afterwards.

A jump is also written when its bytes do not fit the 8 or 16 aligned bytes
the old code needed: one store when they share a cache line. Across two
lines it is written the way the Linux kernel patches its own code: an
invalid one-byte opcode first (its fault is redirected to the replacement),
then the rest, then the first byte, with membarrier(SYNC_CORE) in between.
Silksong's two hottest sites end exactly at a cache line.

A site that still cannot get a jump is reported once it has faulted 100000
times.
---
diff --git a/src/loader/guestInstructionPatcher.cpp b/src/loader/guestInstructionPatcher.cpp
